# Archimedes port status

The Arc2 and Arc4 configurations build RISC OS 3.71 for ARM3, MEMC1a,
IOC/IOEB and VIDC1 hardware. Verification has used an emulated A5000;
operation on other Archimedes models and physical hardware remains unverified.
Morris4 is the compatibility target for RISC PC/A7000 builds.

## Building

Use the existing RISC OS build environment. Select `!EnvArc2` for a 2 MB
image or `!EnvArc4` for a 4 MB image, run the corresponding
`BuildSys.Arc2.!Config` or `BuildSys.Arc4.!Config`, then `!MkSystem` in that
build directory. Configuration regenerates the ignored build makefiles.
The outputs are `Install.Images.2MEG` and `Install.Images.4MEG`.
On a host filesystem their filetyped names end in `,fe5`.

Run headless builds as a Wimp task after the desktop starts, with sufficient
WimpSlot. Check the component logs for assembly errors as well as the final
image result; an older module can otherwise conceal a failed rebuild.

## Power-on self-test

POST is enabled for MEMC1 builds. The original reset loop came from probing
the RISC PC test-link address: setting A23 above the MEMC ROM base exceeds
the ARM2/ARM3 address range and causes an address exception. MEMC uses
A21/A22 aliases for a 2 MB image. With a 4 MB image, it probes a known zero
at ROM end minus 20 bytes, where A21 is high. Test-link probing stays enabled.
The ARM600 test-link path retains its original A23 address.

The MEMC entry header uses ARM2/ARM3 instructions with the extended layout:
ROM length at `&60`, followed by POST vector-table offsets at `&64`, `&68`
and `&6C`. BigSplit1 writes the image length using its existing `&60` patch.
Its additional trailer rule reserves the final 20 bytes from modules and
credits, then writes the zero sentinel before computing checksum and CRCs.
This rule applies to all images. Morris ROM trailer/checksum metadata changes;
Morris4 kernel code and data remain byte-identical. BigSplit2 is not used.

The MEMC POST path also corrects IOC read/write byte lanes, sound DMA end
address and control-register restoration, and the IOEB sound interrupt bit.
ARM600 RAM-test and MMU operations remain conditional on that memory model.
The previously omitted `TestSrc/Mem1MEMC1a` source is included in the tree.

Two separate reset faults are fixed:

* Word-aligning `InitKbdWs` prevents rotated ARM3 word loads from reporting
  spurious CMOS-reset keys and briefly turning the border red after POST.
* Before reading CMOS, MEMC POST tries STOP, releases SDA and sends nine
  clocks, then tries STOP again. It repeats once if SDA is still low.
  Recovery runs even if SDA initially reads high, since that may be a one
  bit in an interrupted read. This follows the bus-clear procedure in
  [NXP UM10204, section 3.1.16](https://www.nxp.com/docs/en/user-guide/UM10204.pdf).
  STOP alone cannot clear a slave that is holding SDA low while transmitting.

The CMOS recovery fixes an intermittent persistent red POST result `&10051`
after hard reset. An application crash is not needed to reproduce it.

### Remaining RAM-test limitation

The MEMC path tests RAM data/address lines and byte strobes, but lacks the
broad RAM pattern sweep present in RISC OS 3.11. The 3.71 bulk-test driver
assumes ARM600 MMU mappings, PhysRamTable and VRAM banks. A MEMC driver still
needs to be restored; skipping this driver is not a MEMC hardware requirement.
The existing ARM600 driver remains in place for Morris builds.

## Display Manager

The legacy implementation enumerates numeric modes 0..127 instead of using
the old dummy table. It checks monitor/memory validity and the Wimp's mode
restrictions, obtains dimensions and depth from kernel mode variables, and
applies the original numeric mode. Non-graphic, double-pixel and modes above
8bpp are excluded. Morris retains the Medusa implementation.

## Verification, 22 October 2026

Arculator configuration: ARM3 33 MHz, MEMC1a 16 MHz, 4 MB RAM.

* The final 4 MB image passed one cold boot and nine hard resets, all with
  green POST result `&51` and no later red border.
* The final 2 MB image passed POST and booted to the desktop.
* Forcing the sentinel to all ones selected forced-test mode and reported
  the expected ROM failure (`&254`). Corrupting the upper ROM at `&300000`
  with the sentinel intact reported ROM failure without forced mode (`&251`).
* Both images passed independent additive-checksum and four-lane CRC checks.
  Synthetic MEMC 2/4 MB and Morris 4 MB images built with the real BASIC
  builder also passed; old/new Morris fixtures differ only in the sentinel,
  additive checksum and CRC fields.
* The I2C recovery passed 444 emulator state/clock/data combinations and
  forced interrupted-read tests, including the read-address ACK case.
* Display Manager exposed 34 usable numeric modes on the tested monitor.
  Applying modes 30, 8 and 15 was verified through its UI.
* A CPU-only reset with IOC's power-on flag clear reached the warm-start
  path and continued executing. Its final screenshot was inconclusive;
  return to the desktop is not established by that test.

Fresh Morris4 builds produced byte-identical kernel and Display Manager
modules compared with the pre-change references. SHA-256 values:

* Kernel: `63339a5d073f8023d1a904779ce9b60daf61d44782e79c9d9feb7b76c01152e8`
* Display: `5b65c9e65095b9a27168f444a149bd07c33dad74148fcd89a0a0926833f5dfef`

This is a generated-code comparison, not a RISC PC hardware boot test.
The separately reported Sparkplug application crash remains undiagnosed.

